  <h1>About</h1>
  <p>
    I'm Alissa Nicolet, an LCA engineer and software builder based in the Netherlands.
    I've sat in every chair around the sustainability-software table: the customer who
    needed a number she could defend, the methodology expert who had to verify it, and
    now the product owner deciding what gets built.
  </p>

  <h2>What I do now</h2>
  <p>
    I'm a product owner for product-footprinting software serving European retailers —
    the tooling layer where regulations like CSRD, ESPR, and digital product passports
    turn into features, data models, and backlog. My job is making sure the numbers the
    software produces are ones a verifier, a customer, and a regulator can all audit.
  </p>

  <h2>How I got here</h2>
  <p>
    I started on the methodology side: life-cycle assessment and EPDs under EN 15804
    and the Dutch NMD system. As a customer of LCA software I kept hitting the same
    wall — numbers nobody could trace back to an assumption. So I started building the
    alternative: tooling where a verifier approves the methodology once and every
    calculation inherits that approval, instead of re-arguing it per project.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most recently that's <strong>LCI·GRAPH</strong>, an open-source pipeline that pulls
    life-cycle inventory data out of academic papers and into a queryable graph — because
    the field's best data is still locked in PDFs.
  </p>

  <h2>How I work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A number nobody can audit is a liability, not a deliverable.</li>
    <li>Predictable beats clever — a user must always be able to explain where a number came from.</li>
    <li>AI drafts, rules decide, humans approve.</li>
    <li>A thin slice that crosses the whole stack proves more than a completed layer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
  <p>
    I write on <a href="https://scop3.substack.com/">scop3</a>. I work in English and Dutch.
    The fastest way to reach me is email or LinkedIn — links below.
  </p>
